        "problem": "${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)(-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$",
